// #110 R3 — ADMIT half (LIVE HOLE). A by-value match EXPRESSION over a SLICE
// element whose binding carries drop glue compiles: `return match s[0] { W { i:
// y } => … }` on `s: &[W]` moves `y` out of borrowed slice storage, so the arm
// drops it and the owner's glue drops it again (one value, two destructor calls).
//
// lower_match_expr's place test (sema_stmt.cpp:9635-9637) lists VarRef /
// FieldRead / TupleIndex / Deref / IndexRead and NOT SliceIndex, so a slice-index
// scrutinee reaches neither the temp-hoist path nor the mark-moved path. Same
// omission as lower_match (stmt form) and mark_match_scrutinee_moved.
//
// ONE-VARIABLE TWIN: the identical program with the parameter spelled `&[W; 1]`
// (array, IndexRead) instead of `&[W]` (slice, SliceIndex) REFUSES today:
//   error [fn f]: cannot move out of type `[W; 1]`, a non-copy array
// Only the scrutinee's index KIND differs; the array half already refuses, the
// slice half is the hole. Fix by DELEGATING to one place predicate, not by
// appending SliceIndex here — the same list is wrong at three other sites.
package bc_match_expr_slice_elem_moved;
struct Inner { }
impl Drop for Inner { fn drop(self: &mut Inner) { } }
struct W { i: Inner }
fn f(s: &[W]) -> i32 { return match s[0] { W { i: y } => 1i32 }; }